---
import Post from '../layouts/Post.astro';
import DeliveryExplorer from '../components/DeliveryExplorer.astro';
import katex from 'katex';
import release from '../../public/cop-prisoner/release.json';
import classificationStatus from '../content/generated/cop-prisoner-classification-status.html?raw';
const math=(s:string)=>katex.renderToString(s,{displayMode:true,throwOnError:true});
---
<Post title="Guaranteed indirect delivery: the exact maximum" description="The maximum number of guaranteed indirect deliveries at each vertex and arc count: explicit attaining constructions, four budget regimes, and the remaining gaps." updated={release.publication_date}>
  <p>A messenger has a message at one station of a directed network. A pursuer starts elsewhere. Both see each other and take turns following an arrow or waiting, with the messenger moving first. Which source–recipient pairs can always complete delivery?</p>
  <p>We count pairs with different endpoints and <strong>no direct arrow</strong> between them. Write <i>I(G)</i> for the number that are guaranteed from every permitted pursuer starting position. The central problem is to determine</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M(n,m)=\max_{|V(G)|=n,\ |E(G)|=m}I(G),\qquad n\ge1,\quad0\le m\le n(n-1),`)} />
  <p>and give an explicit attaining construction at every pair of resources. This means concrete rules for the vertices and arrows, or a specified composition procedure, with all parameters determined from <i>(n,m)</i>. A certified, listed finite graph also qualifies. An existence statement, general graph search, or derandomization procedure alone does not complete this construction requirement. Isolated stations are allowed.</p>
  <p><strong>The complete classification remains open.</strong> The known results determine every budget from 24 onward at every order <i>n ≥ 115</i>, together with many smaller cases and intervals. Their explicit attaining recipes are tracked separately from the numerical values in the <a href="#explicit-constructions">construction inventory</a>.</p>
  <p><a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/">Classification proofs</a> · <a href="/cop-prisoner/paper/">Paper and evidence</a> · <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/">Lean coverage</a> · <a href="/cop-prisoner/delivery-time/">Delivery-time companion</a></p>

  <h2>The rules and the counting bound</h2>
  <p>The graph is finite and directed, without loops or repeated arcs; opposite arrows are allowed. The messenger observes the pursuer's initial position and may adapt its strategy to it. Receipt wins immediately, even when the pursuer occupies the recipient. Every other collision loses, and evading forever does not count as delivery.</p>
  <p>There are <i>L = n(n − 1)</i> possible arrows and <i>h = L − m</i> missing arrows. Each counted pair must be missing, so <i>M(n,m) ≤ h</i>. For <i>n ≥ 3</i>, the additional vertex bound is <i>I(G) ≤ n(n − 3)</i>: a recipient with at most one predecessor can be blocked, while one with at least two has at most <i>n − 3</i> eligible indirect sources.</p>
  <p>For <i>n ≥ 5</i>, four consecutive arc-count regimes organize the problem. Orders one through four belong to the complete finite classification described below; the interval boundaries need not have the same order there.</p>

  <h2 id="sparse-regime">1. Sparse budgets: fewer than 2n arrows</h2>
  <p>Here the strongest constructions use a smaller network and add isolated stations to reach the specified order. For every even budget <i>24 ≤ 2k &lt; 2n</i>,</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M(n,2k)=k(k-3).`)} />
  <p>The attaining network has <i>k</i> stations and two incoming and two outgoing arrows at each. Every pair can deliver. An ordinary composition proof handles every support size from 85, and complete finite certificates supply sizes 12 through 84.</p>
  <p>For every odd budget <i>25 ≤ 2k + 1 &lt; 2n</i>,</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M(n,2k+1)=k(k-3)+2.`)} />
  <p>The construction uses <i>k + 1</i> stations, with precisely controlled degree exceptions. Separately, <i>M(n,23) = 83</i> for every <i>n ≥ 12</i>. These formulas have matching upper bounds and proved attaining graphs. The remaining arc-only budgets 12 through 22 are unresolved, and an arc-only maximum does not by itself settle a host order too small for its attaining graph.</p>
  <p>See the proofs of <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#sparse-endpoint-attainment-at-every-order-from-twelve">the even construction</a>, <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#the-odd-endpoint-and-completion-of-its-degree-defect">the odd construction</a>, and <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#the-exact-23-arc-endpoint">the 23-arrow case</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="main-regime">2. The main interval: every missing pair can win</h2>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M(n,m)=n(n-1)-m,\qquad n\ge115,\quad2n\le m\le n(n-3).`)} />
  <p>Every budget in this interval has an explicit polynomial construction. Sparse repeating networks cover the first budgets; protected routing and incomparable hub sets connect them to dense constructions. Each prescribed optional-arrow list permits every subset, so selecting its size gives the exact required budget. This freedom concerns the specified list; adding an arbitrary arrow can also help the pursuer.</p>
  <p>Several parts begin earlier. The sparse endpoint <i>M(n,2n) = n(n − 3)</i> holds at every <i>n ≥ 12</i>. The dense endpoint <i>M(n,n(n − 3)) = 2n</i> holds at every <i>n ≥ 10</i>. Robust dense envelopes cover every missing count <i>2n ≤ h ≤ 3n</i> at every <i>n ≥ 16</i>, with larger explicit envelopes in further domains. Additional arithmetic and protected-routing families cover other smaller-order intervals.</p>
  <p>The threshold 115 is sufficient; the smallest possible onset is open. Two-move witnesses are essential proof tools for the routing and dense parts, while sparse strategies may take longer. The main counting theorem requires finite delivery and does not claim two moves throughout the interval.</p>
  <figure><img src="/cop-prisoner/figures/antichain-routing.svg" alt="Incomparable missing-hub sets leave an intermediate hub accessible to the source and inaccessible to the pursuer" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Incomparable hub sets supply a safe intermediate station. The construction gives an interval of exact arc counts, with every prescribed outside-arrow subset permitted.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>The <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#an-explicit-threshold-for-the-entire-main-interval">main-interval theorem</a>, <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#composition-through-incomparable-hub-sets">antichain construction</a>, and <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#direct-missing-graphs-unify-the-dense-constructions">dense envelopes</a> state their full hypotheses and smaller-order coverage.</p>

  <h2 id="final-regime">3. The final dense band: some missing pairs must fail</h2>
  <p>Past <i>n(n − 3)</i> arrows, the missing-pair count is no longer attainable. For every <i>n ≥ 20</i>, put <i>m = n(n − 3) + r</i>. The whole remaining nonzero band is exact:</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M\bigl(n,n(n-3)+r\bigr)=2n-r-\left\lceil\frac r2\right\rceil-1,\qquad1\le r\le n.`)} />
  <p>Each value has a proved attaining construction. At the first step, one added arrow leaves <i>2n − 3</i> guarantees; this step already works for every <i>n ≥ 16</i>. At the last step,</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M(n,n(n-2))=\left\lfloor\frac{n-2}{2}\right\rfloor,\qquad n\ge2.`)} />
  <p>Functional blocks and explicit dense cores attain the staircase. Their conditional construction criteria also prove individual smaller-order cases. The complete band below order 20 must be assessed against those criteria and the finite evidence, rather than filled by the eventual formula.</p>
  <figure><img src="/cop-prisoner/figures/dense-staircase.svg" alt="The proved dense-band maximum at 100 stations falls in a staircase from 200 guarantees to 49" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The solid curve is the exact maximum at 100 stations. The dashed line counts missing arrows before accounting for forced failures.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>See the <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#exact-final-dense-band">staircase theorem</a> and its <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#functional-roots-and-the-rest-of-the-staircase">conditional attaining constructions</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="zero-regime">4. Above the final boundary: no indirect guarantee</h2>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M(n,m)=0,\qquad n\ge2,\quad n(n-2)<m\le n(n-1).`)} />
  <p>This range is completely settled. Every graph at these resources has no guaranteed indirect pair, so any graph with the specified number of arrows attains zero. The only graph at order one also has value zero. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#the-dense-boundary">dense-boundary proof</a> explains the obstruction.</p>

  <h2>What the classification still needs</h2>
  <p>Complete joint values are known through six stations. At larger orders, a proved interval establishes exactly its stated resource pairs. The remaining task is to settle all uncovered pairs and supply explicit attaining constructions, including unresolved sparse budgets and smaller-order parts of the main and final bands. Even at arbitrarily large orders, the eventual theorem leaves the unresolved arc budgets 12 through 22.</p>
  <p>There are two questions for each resource pair: whether its exact value is proved, and whether an explicit graph attaining that value is supplied. The <a href="#explicit-constructions">separate construction audit</a> found a concrete recipe or certified listed graph for every currently incorporated exact value, so the known-value construction-only gap set is empty. This conclusion comes from checking the families and their parameters; it does not follow merely from reducing the numerical problem to finitely many cases. Unknown values still need both parts.</p>
  <p>Maximizing over one resource gives the useful consequences <i>M<sub>V</sub>(n) = max<sub>m</sub> M(n,m)</i> and <i>M<sub>E</sub>(m) = max<sub>n</sub> M(n,m)</i>. In particular, <i>M<sub>V</sub>(n) = n(n − 3)</i> for every <i>n ≥ 12</i>, while <i>M<sub>V</sub>(7) = 21</i> and <i>M<sub>V</sub>(8) = 32</i>. The unresolved vertex maxima satisfy <i>48 ≤ M<sub>V</sub>(9) ≤ 53</i>, <i>62 ≤ M<sub>V</sub>(10) ≤ 69</i>, and <i>84 ≤ M<sub>V</sub>(11) ≤ 87</i>. These one-resource conclusions do not determine every joint value.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#the-combined-joint-maximum">combined theorem</a> gives the exact formula for every <i>n ≥ 115</i> and <i>24 ≤ m ≤ n(n − 1)</i>. The proofs retain the additional smaller domains and finite cases separately.</p>

  <Fragment set:html={classificationStatus}/>

  <h2>Try the game and inspect the evidence</h2>
  <DeliveryExplorer />
  <p>The general counting and construction theorems have ordinary proofs. Sparse composition also uses independently checked local certificates and a proof that they apply to every permitted network size. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/paper/#evidence-guide">evidence guide</a> identifies these dependencies. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/">Lean development</a> formalizes the game, selected structural implications, and explicit sparse and dense examples; it does not formalize the complete classification.</p>
  <p>Optimizing the delivery time is a separate, parked companion problem. Its <a href="/cop-prisoner/delivery-time/">proved two-move bounds and partial fast constructions</a> remain available without enlarging the scope of the exact-count classification.</p>

  <h2>Where the work comes from</h2>
  <p>Alexandra Ignatova developed the original problem and layered construction in 2023 under Angel Raychev's mentorship; the retained manuscript is dated February 2024. The <a href="https://uchi.bas.bg/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Knijka_reziumeta_2023.pdf">2023 student research abstracts, page 27</a> record that provenance. Ignatova and Raychev had already established the <i>n(n − 3)</i> upper bound and investigated two-in/two-out constructions.</p>
  <p>The September 2026 work corrects the layered counting argument and develops attaining families, extremal bounds, computational evidence, and formalization with assistance from Astra 6 through Codex. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/paper/">paper page</a> preserves that credit and the unresolved publication status.</p>
</Post>
